#Problem APEX 3.1.12

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Global extrema)
## KEYWORDS('derivative', 'maximum','minimum','relative', 'absolute')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## MLT(calculus_singlevariable_absolut_extrema_graphs)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('3.1')
## Problem1('12')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl',
    'plots.pl',      'parserCheckboxList.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric");
$a  = random(-0.9, -0.2, 0.1);
$b  = random( 3,    6);
$b1 = $b - 1;
$f1 = Compute("x^2");
$f2 = Compute("x^$b");
$c  = random(0.2, 0.5, 0.1);

($x1, $y1) = ($a, $f1->eval(x => $a));
($x2, $y2) = (0, 0);
($x3, $y3) = ($c, $f2->eval(x => $c));

$ans1 = $f1->D('x')->eval(x => $x1);
$ans2 = 0;
$ans3 = $f2->D('x')->eval(x => $x3);

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -1,
    xmax            =>  1,
    xtick_delta     =>  1,
    xminor          =>  4,
    ymin            => -1,
    ymax            =>  1,
    ytick_delta     =>  1,
    yminor          =>  4,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function(
    [ $f1, 'x', -1, 0, color => 'blue' ],
    [ $f2, 'x',  0, 1, color => 'blue' ]
);
$plot->add_point([ $x1, $y1 ], [ $x2, $y2 ], [ $x3, $y3 ]);
$plot->add_label(
    [
        $x1, $y1, 'A',
        v_align => 'bottom',
        h_align => 'left',
        color   => 'red'
    ],
    [
        $x2, $y2, 'B',
        v_align => 'bottom',
        h_align => 'left',
        color   => 'red'
    ],
    [
        $x3, $y3, 'C',
        v_align => 'bottom',
        h_align => 'left',
        color   => 'red'
    ]
);

$alttext =
    "A graph starting at A near x=$a, falling to the origin, labelled B, then "
    . "rising to point C at x=$c, then rising to the edge of the plotting "
    . "window at (1,1).";

$options = [
    [
        "Absolute maximum",
        "Relative maximum",
        "Absolute minimum",
        "Relative minimum"
    ],
    "None of the above"
];

$mc1 = CheckboxList($options, [4]);
$mc2 = CheckboxList($options, [ 2, 3 ]);
$mc3 = CheckboxList($options, [4]);

BEGIN_PGML
The graph of [` f(x) `] is shown below, where
[``
  f(x) = \begin{cases}
    x^2 & x \leq 0 \\
    x^{[$b]} & x > 0.
    \end{cases}
``]

>> [! [$alttext]!]{$plot}{300} <<

Evaluate [`f'(x)`] at the points indicated in the graph and
identify the points as being an absolute maximum or
minimum, a relative maximum or minimum, or none of the above.  
(Select all that apply.)

a. At point [`A = ([$x1], [$y1])`],  [` f'([$x1]) = `] [_]{$ans1}{10}.

b. This means that at point [`A`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc1}

c. At point [`B = ([$x2], [$y2])`],  [` f'([$x2]) = `] [_]{$ans2}{10}.

d. This means that at point [`B`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc2}

e. At point [`C = ([$x3], [$y3])`],  [` f'([$x3]) = `] [_]{$ans3}{10}.

f. This means that at point [`C`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The derivative is 
[```
  f'(x) = \begin{cases}
    2x & x < 0 \\
    [$b] x^{[$b1]} & x > 0.
    \end{cases}
```]
Now since
[```
  \lim_{x \to 0^-} (2 x) =
  \lim_{x \to 0^+} ([$b] x^{[$b1]}) = 0,
```]
the function [`f`] is differentiable at [`x = 0`], with derivative
value [`f'(0) = 0`].  This indicates the possibility of a relative
extremum at [`x = 0`].  According to the graph, there is a
relative and absolute minimum at this point.

There are no extrema at points [`A`] or [`C`] since the
derivative values are nonzero there:
[```
  f'([$x1]) = 2([$x1]) = [$ans1]
```]
and
[```
  f'([$x3]) = [$b]([$x3])^{[$b1]} = [$ans3].
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

